<blog>
<entry when='20261125T074625'>
<title>Human sorting, hardened</title>
<category>gem</category>
<category>python</category>
<category>development</category>

<description>How many times can I revisit three lines of code that are 20 years
old?</description>

<body>

<p>"Human sorting" is sorting strings (usually file names) so that any numbers
in the names come out in the right order. For example, "file10.txt" should come
after "file2.txt", but regular sorting will put them in the wrong order.  I've
written twice before about the function to do this
(<a pref="blog/human-sorting.bx"/> and
<a pref="blog/human-sorting-improved.bx"/>), but there's more to say about
it.</p>

<p>One effect of AI these days is bug reports for obscure edge cases. As a
maintainer, it's been a challenge first to read through them all, and then to
decide which ones deserve a fix. It's tempting to fix every little thing no
matter how unlikely it is to happen in the real world. But every fix costs time,
energy and focus. Every fix comes with risks in the form of new bugs or changed
behavior. So some long-tail issues should be marked as "won't fix."</p>

<p>But this one intrigued me: if a file name includes a numeric component with
4301 digits, then the human sorting function fails. Buh?</p>

<p>Before we get to the current problem, here's the original "buggy" code,
slightly simplified:</p>

<code lang="python"><![CDATA[
def human_key(s: str) -> tuple[list[str | int], str]:
    """Turn a string into a sortable value that works how humans expect.

    "z23A" -> (["z", 23, "A"], "z23A")

    """
    def try_int(s: str) -> str | int:
        """If `s` is a number, return an int, else `s` unchanged."""
        try:
            return int(s)
        except ValueError:
            return s

    parts = re.split(r"(\d+)", s)
    split_key = [try_int(c) for c in parts]
    return (split_key, s)

def human_sorted(strings: Iterable[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Sort the given iterable of strings the way that humans expect."""
    return sorted(strings, key=human_key)
]]></code>

<p>It splits the string into parts alternating between runs of digits and runs
of non-digits.  It uses <c>try_int</c> on all of them, which turns the digits
into ints and leaves the non-digits as strings. That gives us a list of words
and numbers that can be compared to do the sort we need.</p>

<p>The problem now is with that 4301-digit run. Since 2022, Python's
string-to-integer conversion has had a limit on the number of digits that can be
converted. This is to prevent denial of service attacks based on the quadratic
time to convert a string to an int.  The default limit is 4300 digits. If a
string is longer than that, <c>int()</c> raises a ValueError.</p>

<p>But we were counting on ValueError to mean "this isn't an integer", and to
separate words from digits. Now that too-long number makes <c>try_int</c> return
the string un-converted, and using it as a sort key will try to compare an int
to a string, failing with:</p>

<code><![CDATA[
TypeError: '<' not supported between instances of 'int' and 'str'
]]></code>

<p>Of course, it's absurd to be concerned that an actual file name will include
a run of more than 4300 digits. But I've been tweaking this function over
decades, and I like fiddling with it.  The <a urlid="comments">comments on the
original post</a> offered a number of alternative implementations, a few of
which would have avoided this problem.</p>

<url id="comments" href="/blog/200712/human_sorting#comments"/>

<p>Rather than try to convert all of the parts of the name, we know from how
<c>re.split</c> works that the parts will alternate between words and numbers,
starting with a word even if it's just an empty string.  So we can attempt
conversion only for the parts that we know are digits.</p>

<p>First, we replace <c>try_int</c> with a function to safely convert digit
spans into numbers:</p>

<code lang="python"><![CDATA[
def number(s: str) -> float:
    """Convert s to a number with no errors."""
    try:
        return int(s)
    except ValueError:
        # This only happens if the number is too large to convert to an int.
        return float("inf")
]]></code>

<p>Now we can create <c>split_key</c> differently by only converting the
odd-numbered parts:</p>

<code lang="python"><![CDATA[
split_key = [(number(p) if i % 2 else p) for i, p in enumerate(parts)]
]]></code>

<p>It's tempting to check whether a part starts with a digit:</p>

<code lang="python"><![CDATA[
split_key = [(number(p) if p[:1].isdigit() else p) for p in parts] 
]]></code>

<p>But <c>isdigit()</c> gets us into Unicode difficulties because <c>\d</c> and
<c>isdigit()</c> disagree on what count as digits.  Something odd like
<c>"a1²b"</c> would be split into <c>['a', '1', '²b']</c>, and
<c>'²'.isdigit()</c> is true, so now we'd be mistaking words for numbers.</p>

<p>Another even-odd approach, but it's too fancy:</p>

<code lang="python"><![CDATA[
converters = itertools.cycle([str, number])
split_key = [fn(part) for fn, part in zip(converters, parts)]
]]></code>

<p>In the end, though this is a long-tail bug, I fixed the human sort function;
it was a tiny isolated fix. Also, I don't like having a function that works only
99.9999% of the time. And maybe it's still not done: there are probably other
odd edge cases that could be a problem here. If I hear of those, I might make
more fixes.</p>

<p>The AI fire-hose of issues and pull requests requires me to exercise and
strengthen my "won't fix" muscle. But I also get to choose where to make a fix
just because it pleases me.</p>

<p><b>Update:</b> <a urlid="stylus">Jeff Epler suggested</a> a few clever
alternatives.  The 4300-digit limit is only applied to base 10 conversion. If we
interpret digit spans as base 16, there's no limit on length, and the resulting
ints will sort the same as their base 10 counterparts.  No helper function is
needed at all.  As an added bonus, there are now no floats, so the type hinting
can be more straighforward.  The result is satisfyingly compact:</p>

<code lang="python"><![CDATA[
def _human_key(s: str) -> tuple[list[str | int], str]:
    """Turn a string into a list of string and number chunks.

    "z23a" -> (["z", 35, "a"], "z23a")

    Runs of digits are converted using base 16 because it has no digit limit,
    and the numbers will sort the same as if they were interpreted as base 10.
    The original string is appended as a last value to ensure the key is unique
    enough so that "x1y" and "x001y" can be distinguished.
    """
    parts = re.split(r"(\d+)", s)
    split_key = [(int(p, base=16) if i % 2 else p) for i, p in enumerate(parts)]
    return (split_key, s)

def human_sorted(strings: Iterable[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Sort the given iterable of strings the way that humans expect.

    Numeric components in the strings are sorted as numbers.

    Returns the sorted list.

    """
    return sorted(strings, key=_human_key)
]]></code>

<url id="stylus" href="https://social.afront.org/@stylus/117679145301569683" />

<p>Another of their suggestions was to use a different int substitute. We don't
need actual ints in the key, just something that will sort the same way as ints.
Their idea was to turn digit runs into a tuple of (length, value) with no
integer conversion at all!</p>

<code lang="python"><![CDATA[
split_key = [
    (
        (len(p2 := p.lstrip("0")), p2)
        if i % 2 else p
    )
    for i, p in enumerate(parts)
]
]]></code>

<p>I haven't done timings of any of these solutions. This last solution avoids
the quadratic time of integer conversion, but would likely be slower for the
real-world data we find in file names.</p>

</body>
